## What happened

`DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS because Apple's security frameworks **do not support DSA**. .NET on macOS relies on the platform's crypto libraries, and Apple removed DSA support. This has been the case since .NET 5+ on macOS — if it worked on .NET 10 it was likely through a compatibility shim or OpenSSL-based fallback that was removed in .NET 11.

## How to fix it

**Option 1 — Use BouncyCastle (recommended for verification-only):**

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] data, byte[] signature, byte[] publicKeyBytes)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
    signer.Init(false, keyParam);
    signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

**Option 2 — Use OpenSSL via a runtime check (platform-conditional):**

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    // Use BouncyCastle or a bundled OpenSSL interop
}
else
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // Windows/Linux still support DSA via CNG/OpenSSL
}
```

**Option 3 — Migrate away from DSA entirely.** DSA is considered legacy/weak. If you control both signing and verification, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, which are supported on all platforms.

## Summary

| Platform | DSA support in .NET 11 |
|----------|----------------------|
| Windows  | ✅ (via CNG)         |
| Linux    | ✅ (via OpenSSL)     |
| macOS    | ❌ (`PlatformNotSupportedException`) |

The cleanest long-term fix is migrating to ECDSA/Ed25519. If you must verify legacy DSA signatures, use BouncyCastle as a cross-platform software implementation.
